. * ============================
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2
. * ============================
. 
. * H2: Having daughters leads to more left-leaning political preferences. 
. 
. * To empirically test this hypothesis, an ordered logistic regression (ologit
> ) model is employed. The dependent variable (leftleaning) captures political 
> preferences on a scale from right-wing to left-wing, based on respondents' pa
> rty preference.
. 
. * The main independent variable of interest is a binary indicator (d_daughter
> ) representing whether respondents have at least one daughter (1) or none (0)
> . Control variables include respondents' gender, age, number of children, hou
> sehold size, years of education, labor income, migration background, family s
> tatus, region, and survey year, with interactions between region and survey y
> ear to account for temporal and regional variations. Standard errors are clus
> tered at the household level to account for within-household correlations.
. 
. * The coefficient estimate for d_daughter specifically tests whether responde
> nts with daughters exhibit significantly more left-leaning political preferen
> ces compared to those without daughters.
.         
. ologit leftleaning d_daughter male age num_kids hhsize pgbilzeit pglabgro i.m
> igback i.pgfamstd eastgermany##i.syear, cluster(hid)

